 ## Acceptance criteria
 
 ### Checked
-<!-- ACn: statement. Each maps 1:1 to a `check "ACn: ..."` line in check.sh. Deterministic. -->
+- AC1: An issue's raft is its own `raft:` line, written in the rafting worktree. `new raft` over prepared issues and `fold` publish the raft's claim alone (a fresh `preparing-…` stamp, remembered in this checkout), never restamp a prepared issue, refuse new and prepared issues together, a solo issue with others, another project, another type and a raft that is not in the backlog, and `fold` resumes only its own claim. The carried `status <R> prepared` takes prepared members as they are, takes a `preparing` member only with R's own stamp or a claim this checkout made, refuses a member another raft holds on origin, and, for a raft z-raft grouped, needs its Theme and Order & dependencies. z-design's claim refuses an issue already in a raft, and `ready` refuses a raft while a member is `preparing`. A raft records a hash of each prepared member's design files it grouped (`raftplan.isDesignFile`), and the carried move and `ready` refuse a member whose design changed since, a member this rafting grouped with no entry, and (with several members) a raft none of whose `design-review-NNN.md` files quotes a `Grouped:` record covering the current one (an `unfold` alone needs none). Every hash reads git's view of the files its own commit carries: the carried move and `ready` read the tree they publish, the carried move's raft reviews read tracked and untracked files, ignored ones left out, and `compact`, `remap` and `renumber` hash the index. So neither an ignored file nor an uncommitted work file ever changes a hash. `unfold` takes members out of a backlog raft. `release` also gives back the issue claims this checkout made. Two raftings of one issue conflict on its `raft:` line. A published raft move never restamps a prepared member and refuses one another raft holds. `release` gives back exactly what carries the stamp (prepared when its design is ready, else new), only for this checkout's claim unless `--force`, without merging; the orphan it leaves is an audit warning.
+- AC2: `recombine <keeper> <other> [<issue>...]` moves the named members, or all, under one shared stamp on both rafts, retires the other only when it ends up empty (into `rafts/retired/`, keeping its review history for `lessons`, `metrics` and `audit`), merges `origin/main` before it reads membership, keeps every reader that walks rafts green on a tree holding a retired raft (c0114/classified, c0043/rounds, `lessons`, `audit`, `engine.empty`), refuses another project, another type, an active, done or claimed raft, a non-member and a solo issue with others, and both rafts are readyable after the merge.
+- AC3: With no argument, z-raft's plan never mixes types or projects, folds only into prepared backlog rafts that aren't single (defects into the lowest open defect raft that fits, chores likewise), groups features only by a shared surface, honours the 1,500-line cap inclusively, keeps solo and over-cap issues alone, skips an issue with no `size:` and never folds into a raft with a member that has none, and is the same for any input order.
+- AC4: `raft-plan` prints that plan as commands built from the documents (Touched modules, `size:`, `solo:`, roots, raft states and `single:`), then the skips or "nothing to raft", only reads, and `--apply` runs the commands in a worktree only.
+- AC5: z-design claims, reviews, spends and prepares one issue with no raft; `ready` sends a raftless issue to z-raft; `metrics` counts design rounds per issue, rolls them up to the raft, and sets `size:` against the changed lines.
+- AC6: z-design, z-raft, zz-rules, zz-review, zz-stats, z-ready, the README, AGENTS.md and the usage say what §4 to §9 say.
+- AC7: `promote-check <issue>` copies the issue's own acceptance specs, says how many, fails on one that breaks the type check, and removes the copies (run in a clone under `.cache`).
+- AC8: the real tree passes `./zn skills --check`, `./zn lessons --check` and `./zn audit` with z-raft added.
+- AC9: the promoted claim, prep, start, ready, stop, board-refresh and remap specs, and c0115's overridden README spec, stay green.
+- AC10: `compact`, `remap` and `renumber` re-record every grouped entry that matched before their rewrite (raft document and quoted reviews), so `ready` takes a raft nothing redesigned, and a record that was stale stays stale.
 
